/E (To navigate this issue effectively, delegates must establish precise legal terminology, distinguishing between terms that carry distinct legal definitions, rights, and obligations under international law. Strictly defined by the 1951 UN Convention Relating to the Status of Refugees and its 1967 Protocol, a refugee is an individual who, owing to a well-founded fear of being persecuted for reasons of race, religion, nationality, membership of a particular social group, or political opinion, is outside the country of their nationality and is unable or unwilling to avail themselves of the protection of that country. The 1951 Convention explicitly prohibits non-refoulement, which is the practice of returning refugees or asylum seekers to countries where their life or freedom would be threatened. In contrast, according to the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees \(UNHCR\), an asylum seeker is an individual who has crossed an international border seeking sanctuary and international protection, but whose claim to refugee status has not yet been formally processed or determined by the host state. While not every asylum seeker is ultimately granted refugee status, all asylum seekers are entitled to due process and fundamental human rights protections while their claims are evaluated. Furthermore, while there is no universally binding international legal definition for a migrant, the United Nations widely understands )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
